# ALLOWLIST, for the same reason as the tenant image's. See that file.
#
# waku/ is NOT admitted, and that is the load-bearing line: it is what makes a
# `python -c "import waku"` in services.Dockerfile a build failure rather than
# a boundary violation nobody notices. README.md is not admitted either,
# which is why the Dockerfile passes --no-install-project.
*
.*

!hosted/
!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
!LICENSE
!LICENSE-BRAND

**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/

# Secrets, re-excluded INSIDE the admitted trees. The lines above this block
# admit whole directories, and an allowlist that only names top-level entries
# cannot see a waku/.env or a skills/deploy.pem that somebody wrote while
# debugging -- it rides in with the tree that admits it. These four patterns
# are the only part of this file that is not about build noise, and they must
# stay BELOW the `!` lines: Docker is last-match-wins, so the same four lines
# above the admissions would be undone by them.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
